      "textEn": "25\nGod is situated as Nar-Narayan Archavigraha, this is one of the reasons for giving advice. The man who is spiritually advanced can receive direct inspiration from the temple-archaivigraha. In this way, a devotee of God always takes refuge in the recognized temple of God to make significant progress in divine knowledge and by the grace of God. Even after being filled with the virtues of the Lord,\nTherefore, may the world be sustained by my support. 31st. Meaning of the word\n\nN-- No; Uddhava: Uddhava; Anu-Ranchbhar: Anu-Ranchbhar; Api-- Also; Mat--From me; Nun--Not at all; Arditah--Affected; Prabhuh--Lord; Atha--Therefore; Mat--Without; Mam--Mine; Lokam--The world; Grahayan--To perceive; Iha--In this world; Tishtathu--May it remain;\n\nUddhava is not diminished from me in any way, because he is never by the qualities of nature\nNot affected ¥ | Therefore, to spread the specific knowledge of God, he remains in this world. Meaning: The specific qualification that is required to become a representative of God is:\nTo remain unaffected by the qualities of nature. In the physical world, the highest qualification for a human being is to become a Brahmin.\nBut, since a Brahmin is virtuous, therefore, being a Brahmin alone is not enough to become a representative of God.\nA human being has to transcend the virtuous qualities and be situated in pure essence, which is not influenced by any of the\nqualities of nature. This state of divine capability is called pure essence or Vasudeva.\nAnd in this state, one can experience the knowledge of God. Just as God is not influenced by the qualities of nature,\nsimilarly, a pure devotee of God is also not influenced by the qualities of nature. This is the primary qualification for being in touch with God.\nThe person who attains this divine quality,\nis called liberated while living, although he remains in a physical state. This liberation is attained by him\nwho is constantly engaged in the divine love of God. In Bhaktirasamrita-Sindhu (1.2.187),\nit is said--\n\nHere, by whose actions, mind, and speech, Hari (God) is pleased."
