      "textEn": "33\nIn this world, there is more suffering.\nWhere is the Lord? 2). Meaning of the word:\n\nVidur said: For happiness, to attain happiness; Karmani--desired actions; Karoti--everyone does;\nLokah--in this world; Na--never; Taah--through those actions; Sukhah--no happiness; Va--or else; Anyat--differently;\nUparamam--satisfaction; Va--or else; Vindet--obtains; Bhuyah--contradictory to this; Tatah--by these actions; Eva- certainly;\nDukkham--sorrow; Yat--that; Atra--here; Yuktam- the right path; Bhagavan--Oh great one; Vadeet--please illuminate;\nWhat did he say?: Hey Maharishi, every person in this world engages in desire-driven actions to attain happiness,\nbut neither does he find satisfaction nor does his sorrow diminish.\nOpposite.\nHis sorrow increases from such actions. Therefore, please guide us regarding this, how one can live for true happiness? Meaning: Vidura asked Motreya a few very general questions, but that was not their intention. Uddhav had asked Vidur to go to Maitreya Muni and ask about all the truths of God's name, fame, virtues, form, pastimes, associates, etc.\nTherefore, when Vidur reached Maitreya,\nhe should have only asked about God. But out of natural humility, he did not ask about\na breath of God, but rather asked a question on a subject that could be of great importance to the common people. No ordinary man could have done this to God. First of all, he should know the real state of his life,\nsubject to Maya.\nComing into delusion, man thinks that he can only be happy through\ndesirable actions, but what happens is that man gets more entangled in the web of actions and the fruits of actions\nand does not find any solution to the problem of life. In this\ncontext, there is a beautiful song: 'Because of the great desire to attain all the happiness of life, I built this house.'\nBut unfortunately, all the plans were ruined because an unexpected fire broke out in the house.\nTE\" Nature's law is like that. Every person wants to be happy by making plans and living in the physical world,\nbut the law of nature is so cruel that it sets fire to that plan. A desire-driven person\ndoes not remain happy with his plans, nor does he find any satisfaction from his constant running around for happiness."
