      "textEn": "40\nFor scholars and devotees, they are ever-new, and even devotees like Vidur are not satisfied by the daily recitation of verses from the Bhagavad Gita and Srimad Bhagavat. Vidur, before meeting Maitreya, must have listened to the Lord's\nleelas countless times, but he still wanted to repeat those stories\nbecause he could not be satisfied by listening to them. Of the glorious pastimes of God,\nthe divine nature is just like that. Yayastatvabhedairadhilokanatho\nLokanalokansah Lokapalan.\nAchikripatra hi sarvasatva-\nNikayabhedah adhikrutah pratitu nen\nShabdartha\n\nYa- by whom; Tatva- truth; Bhedaih- by difference; Adhiloka- lord of the worlds; Lokan- to the worlds;\nAlokan- to the worlds of the lower parts; Sah- with; Lokapaalan- to their kings; Achikripat- of planning; Yatra- in which;\nHi- certainly; Sarvas- all; Sattva- existence; Nikaya- living being; Bhedah- by difference; Adhikrutah- authority established;\nPratitu nen- it seems like this. The supreme king of all kings created various worlds and abodes where\nall living beings are situated according to the qualities of nature and actions, and he also created their various kings and\nrulers.\n\nMeaning: Lord Krishna is the head of all kings. He has created different realms for all kinds of creatures.\nIn this world, different types of people have different\ndifferent places for residence. ©. In this, there are places like deserts, snowy regions, and mountainous areas, and in each of them, different kinds of people with different qualities are born according to their past deeds. ©. People are in the Arabian\ndesert and in the valleys of the Himalayan mountains, and the inhabitants of these two places are different from each other.\nJust as the people of the snowy regions are also different from them. There are different kinds of people too. From the earth,\nThe worlds below the netherworld are full of various kinds of creatures. No world is empty, as\nModern so-called scientists imagine due to delusion ©. In the Bhagavad Gita, we find God saying\nthat the soul is omnipresent, that is, present in every field of life. Therefore, there is no doubt that\nin other worlds also, there are residents of our kind, sometimes more intelligent and more knowledgeable than us"
