      "textEn": "4] The conditions for those more intelligent to live are more luxurious than this earth. There are also people where sunlight does not reach, and there are also creatures who have to live there because of their past\ndeeds. Such plans for living conditions have been made by God.\nWent. With the intention of shedding more light on this, Vidur requested Maitreya to describe it.\nRequested. Yena prajnanamut atmakarma-\nRupabhidhanam cha bhidan vyadhatte. Narayano vishchasugatmayonini-\nRetachch no varnaya vipravarya. 9>. Meaning of the word:\n\nYen- from which; Prajanaamu- of the people; Ut- as; Atma- self; Karma- action;\nAbhidhaanam- effort; Cha- also; Bhidaam- within; Vyadhatta- scattered; Narayanah- Narayana; Vishwasrik- creator of the universe;\nAtma-yoni- self-dependent; Etat- all these; Cha- also; Nah- from us; Varnay- describe; Vipra- venerable; Hey Brahmana- O best of Brahmins.\n.\n\nOh, best of Brahmins, you also tell us how the self-reliant Lord Narayana, the creator of the universe,\nhas created the distinct arrangement of the nature, functions, forms, characteristics, and names of various living beings. Meaning: Every living being, according to the qualities of nature, is under the plan of its inherent tendencies. His actions are expressed according to the three qualities of nature, and his form and physical\ncharacteristics are shaped according to his actions, and his name is given according to his physical form.\nIs given. For example, people of upper caste are Sheth (white) and people of lower caste are Kala. This division of white and black is according to the dark and light tasks of human life. Through sacred deeds, a person is born into a good and noble family, he becomes wealthy, becomes a scholar,\nand attains a beautiful physical form.\nAs a result of impure deeds, the condition of the human race\nbecomes poor, always remains in want, remains foolish or uneducated, and gets a ugly body. Vidur requested Maitreya to describe all these inner aspects of the creatures created by Lord Narayana."
